{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## DECISION-3B5F-A: **DELETE**. ADR 0028 para 2's discrimination IS implemented elsewhere — twice, both live. Plus a finding the brief did not have.\n\nAC #1 asks whether the direct-GET confirmation \"has since been implemented elsewhere\". It has. Two\nindependent live implementations, both in the same pass as the dead arm:\n\n### Citation 1 — `outbound_differ._safe_get_issue` (ADR 0028 L13)\n\n`outbound_differ.py:142` maps `HTTPError.code == 404` → the `_DELETED` sentinel\n(`outbound_differ.py:91`), consumed at `outbound_differ.py:693`. This is the bounded direct GET the\nADR's L13 names, and the ADR cites this very module (`outbound_differ.py:73-135,450-644`) as its home.\n\n### Citation 2 — `binding_walk`, the ADR para 4 binding-driven loop\n\n`binding_walk.py:187-196` performs the bounded GET for every off-snapshot binding and maps it to the\nfour-way state the ADR's Consequences describe:\n\n    got is _DELETED         -> ObservedJira.CONFIRMED_404\n    got is _TRANSPORT_ERROR -> ObservedJira.TRANSPORT_ERROR\n    HTTP 200                -> ObservedJira.PRESENT (alive but out of window)\n    client is None          -> ObservedJira.ABSENT_IN_WINDOW (defer, never deletion)\n\n`classify.py:59` defines `CONFIRMED_404`; `classify.py:79` defines `RETIRE_AFTER_GRACE`. It reuses\n`outbound_differ`'s sentinels **unchanged** (stated at `binding_walk.py:132`), and\n`binding_walk.py:281-299` advances grace / retires **only** on `CONFIRMED_404`, with an explicit\n\"never on ABSENT_IN_WINDOW (ADR 0028 §2)\".\n\n**Both are live.** `run_differs.py:222` calls the dead `compute_mutations`; `run_differs.py:257` calls\n`_run_differs_binding_walk` — same function, same pass.\n\n### The ADR itself assigns this to the outbound side, which makes the inbound arm architecturally wrong\n\nL16 shares a `200` overlay with the inbound differ but excludes `404`s and transport errors\n**deliberately**, \"so a gone issue is never inbound-mirrored (**retirement stays outbound-owned**)\".\nAn `(inbound, probe)` arm whose whole purpose is to route a confirmed 404 into the inbound path\ncontradicts that ownership split. The chain is a vestige of the design L13/L16 replaced, not an\nunfinished part of the current one.\n\n### The finding the brief did not have: c244's same-pass re-create is ALSO dead\n\n`route_inbound_probe` (`reconcile_helpers.py:296-334`) is the **sole** producer of `(inbound, delete)`\nin the engine — verified by searching every `MutationAction.delete` construction: the only other one\nis `run_differs.py:588`, which is `direction=outbound`. And `route_inbound_probe` takes an\n`(inbound, probe)` Mutation as its input.\n\nSo with no `(inbound, probe)` producer: `route_inbound_probe` never runs → `(inbound, delete)` is never\nproduced → `_apply_inbound_delete` (`apply_inbound.py:175`) never runs → the\n`create_after_hard_delete` follow-on (`apply_inbound.py:220`) is never emitted.\n\n**That is an ADR-documented Consequence that is currently inert:** \"Confirmed hard-delete of a\nstill-locally-present ticket now re-creates it (c244) … This closes the former `epic-3e36` gap where\nthe follow-on was built but never consumed.\" The follow-on is, once again, never consumed — for a\ndifferent reason than in `epic-3e36`.\n\nI am recording this rather than letting DELETE quietly take it, because it is the one real cost of\ndeleting and it was not in the brief.\n\n### Why it is still DELETE, and what is actually lost\n\nThe **outcome** survives; only the **immediacy** is lost. On a confirmed 404 `binding_walk` retires the\nbinding after grace (`note_absent_or_rekey`, `binding_walk.py:294-299`). Once retired, the local\nticket is unbound, and the normal unbound-local → outbound-create path re-creates the Jira issue.\nSo a hard-deleted issue whose local ticket is still present is re-created **eventually** (after grace),\nwhere c244 promised **same-pass**.\n\nWIRE would buy back that immediacy but at a bad price: it means migrating `run_differs` to pass real\nlocal state AND removing `drop_snapshot_differ_local_state_emissions`, which re-enables the snapshot\ndiffer's `field_drift` and `unbound_local` arms (`reconcile_helpers.py:505-511`). Those arms now\n**duplicate** work `outbound_differ` (local↔remote diff) and `binding_walk` (drift classes A and B)\nalready do — and `binding_walk.py:120-123` states the division of labour explicitly, skipping\n`active_local_ids` because the field differ owns them. Re-lighting a legacy `(prev, curr)` arm to\nrecover one property, while it double-emits two others, is a worse change than restoring that property\nwhere the 404 is already in hand.\n\n**Follow-up filed** for restoring same-pass re-create inside `binding_walk` — a much smaller and\ncorrectly-placed change, since `CONFIRMED_404` + the local ticket are both already available there.\n\n### Scope of the deletion\n\nRemoved: the producer `differ._compute_mutations_emit_absent_partner_probes`, the dispatch arm\n`_run_differs_inbound_probe_dispatch`, `inbound_probe.py` (43 lines — `ProbeBranch`, `ProbeResult`,\n`ProbeConfigError`), `route_inbound_probe` + its re-export, `backend.probe_remote` and the\n`SupportsAbsenceProbe` capability, and the fixture-only\n`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/diffing/test_differ_emits_probe.py` (AC #3).\n\n**`drop_snapshot_differ_local_state_emissions` STAYS.** The ticket's \"what is NOT the fix\" trap applies\nto WIRE: the suppression exists because the caller passes the legacy `(prev, curr)` pair, and DELETE\ndoes not change the caller, so the suppression remains exactly as correct as it is today. Removing it\nhere would un-suppress two unsound arms — the opposite of the trap, and just as wrong.\n\n`_apply_inbound_delete` and its `typed_dispatch` entry are **kept**, orphaned deliberately, with a\ncomment naming the follow-up. Deleting c244's implementation in the same change would make restoring\nsame-pass re-create a rewrite rather than a re-wire."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785868507791210001,"uuid":"37bb5154-28a0-491a-8364-a5da6085cce6"}